The "fruit" that the text is talking about is the result or the consequence of something that happened before. Here it says, "The fame of this work imparted a new impetus to encyclopedic writing, which bore abundant fruit in the subsequent centuries of the Middle Ages." This means that Saint Isidore of Seville's famous work, the Etymologiae, inspired a lot of other people to write encyclopedias during the Middle Ages. Just like a tree that bears fruit, Saint Isidore's work produced a result - in this case, more encyclopedias!
[The End of Assistant 1’s Answer]
